30:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-14","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-fg mb-6","children":"Common questions"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"Why does my SaaS landing page get clicks but no trial signups?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"The most common causes are message mismatch between the ad and the H1, a demo CTA on a product that should offer a self-serve trial, missing product UI above the fold, and no proof adjacent to the primary action. Each of these is observable in the page HTML - an automated audit surfaces which ones apply."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"Should a B2B SaaS page use a demo CTA or a trial CTA?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"It depends on the product complexity and sales motion. Products below ~$200/month ACV with self-serve onboarding convert better with a trial CTA. Products requiring implementation, admin setup, or team rollout benefit from a demo. Mismatching the CTA to the actual sales motion is the most common SaaS landing page mistake."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"What does \"ICP clarity\" mean on a landing page?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"ICP clarity means the headline explicitly identifies who the product is for. \"The all-in-one workspace\" has zero ICP clarity. \"The project management tool for engineering teams\" has it. Cold paid traffic - arriving from an ad that targeted a specific audience - expects the page to confirm immediately that they are in the right place."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"Why do SaaS pages score low on social proof signals?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"Most SaaS pages put social proof below the fold or rely on logo strips without context. Logos alone do not reduce perceived risk for cold traffic. A named quote with a specific outcome (e.g., \"Reduced onboarding time from 4 weeks to 3 days\") outperforms a logo strip every time."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"What is the biggest H1 mistake on SaaS homepages?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"Writing the H1 as a product category rather than a customer outcome. \"Collaborative document platform\" describes the software. \"Write, review, and ship docs without the 14-tab workflow\" describes the customer's situation. The second version is faster to evaluate for cold traffic."}]]}]]}]]}]
